k and direct write paths, they all explicitly remove the temp file after the operation completes, so the defer cleanup is just a safety net for edge cases. The key thing is making sure `clearTempFile` gets called right after a successful write so the signal handler doesn't try to clean up a file that's already been renamed to the output location.\n\nBut there's a wrinkle with the combined temp file case—when `prependOriginal` creates a new temp file and removes the old one, I need to update `tempFilePath` to point to the new file, otherwise the defer cleanup will try to remove a file that no longer exists. ale\n\nIn my new design, `run` has `defer cleanupCurrentTempFile()` which checks the global `tempFilePath`. So:\n1. readAllStdin creates temp A, setTempFile(A) → tempFilePath = A\n2. prependOriginal creates B, removes A → but tempFilePath still = A!\n3. We need to setTempFile(B) in runOutput\n\nIn `runOutput`, after calling prependOriginal, I update the tracking to point to the new temp file if one was created.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the edge cases. If prependOriginal returns data in memory with no temp file, that's fine because it only happens when stdin was already in memory, so there was nothing to track in the first place. The tricky case is when stdin was spilled to disk but the combined result stays in memory—but that doesn't happen because if we had to spill stdin, we'll keep using temp files for the combined data too.\n\nThere's one potential race condition: `combineTempFiles` removes the old stdin temp file, but the signal handler might try to clean it up between that removal and when we update the tracking with the new path. However, removing a non-existent file is harmless, so this is benign.\n\nTo be extra safe, I could update the temp file tracking inside `combineTempFiles` before removing the old file, so the mutex-protected update happens atomically with the removal. Then I need to add `path/filepath` to the imports and write out the complete file.\n\nWriting the full implementation... Writing the main logic... Now I'm handling stdin input by reading all data and checking if it exceeds a memory threshold—if it does, I spill the excess to a temporary file instead of keeping it in memory. Writing temp file management... Writing file handling logic... Writing file I/O utilities... Writing file operations... Finishing the directWrite function and then moving on to handle stdout output, where I check if there's a temp file to copy or just write the data directly to standard output. I'm realizing I need to trace through the cleanup logic more carefully. When `writeOutputFile` calls `spillToTempFile` and then `attemptAtomicWrite`, if the atomic write fails, the temp file persists but gets cleaned up by the defer in `run` since `setTempFile` was already called. However, I need to verify what happens if `spillToTempFile` itself fails partway through. I'm verifying the non-goals are met: the implementation uses lstat to avoid following symlinks on the output path itself, though it does follow them when actually writing (which is correct), no new flags were added, and atomic writes only apply to named files, not stdout. Now checking that all functions stay under 40 lines—run, runPassthrough, readAllStdin are all well under that limit, and runOutput comes in around 24 lines.\n\nAll the helper functions check out too: spillToTempFile, installCleanupHandler, prependOriginal, combineTempFiles, atomicFallbackCopy, and directWrite are all within bounds, with the smaller utilities like setTempFile and clearTempFile being just a few lines each.\n\nThe total comes to around 280 lines, well under the 500-line limit. Now I'm ready to write out the complete file.\n\nActually, let me verify the outputFileInfo return values are being used correctly throughout. It returns mode, exists, isRegular, and error, and I need to make sure every caller is handling all three boolean flags properly. In runOutput I'm passing all of them to writeOutputFile, and the function signature accepts them all correctly.\n\nOne more consideration: prependOriginal uses os.ReadFile which follows symlinks, but since I've already verified isRegular before calling it, I know the file is definitely a regular file at that point. And for the non-goal about not following symlinks on output—the lstat check catches symlinks and prevents append mode from triggering, so that's handled. Though I should double-check whether directWrite's os.OpenFile behavior is consistent with this approach.\n\nActually, os.OpenFile does follow symlinks when writing, which matches what the C implementation does—we inspect the path with lstat (no following) but write to it normally (which follows). That's the correct behavior and aligns with the non-goal of operating on the path as given.\n\nNow I can write the complete file.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5747,"cache_read_input_tokens":161069,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5747},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f990e60-8242-4d19-ac3a-f605f702f984","uuid":"80bcb2c9-32c1-403c-8ceb-a9642a0e38e5"}
